1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,956
Add an AI Agent node after your Sheets node, attach your Gemini model, and open the

2
00:00:04,956 --> 00:00:05,677
Prompt field.

3
00:00:05,677 --> 00:00:10,061
The User Message hands over every row that made it through your filter: client,

4
00:00:10,061 --> 00:00:12,170
contact, renewal date, days remaining.

5
00:00:12,170 --> 00:00:16,554
You are not writing the email yourself, you are handing the agent the facts and

6
00:00:16,554 --> 00:00:19,384
trusting the system message to shape them properly.

7
00:00:19,384 --> 00:00:22,270
Why does tone need engineering when content did not?

8
00:00:22,270 --> 00:00:26,876
Because left to their defaults, models write in a voice no real firm uses: a little

9
00:00:26,876 --> 00:00:30,649
too enthusiastic, a little too padded, delighted to be following up.

10
00:00:30,649 --> 00:00:35,089
Your clients know how your company sounds, and a chase that suddenly sounds like

11
00:00:35,089 --> 00:00:37,531
software is a chase that gets screenshotted.

12
00:00:37,531 --> 00:00:42,026
The standard to hold every draft to is simple and personal: read it aloud and ask

13
00:00:42,026 --> 00:00:45,855
whether you would send it, word for word, under your own name, today.

14
00:00:45,855 --> 00:00:47,131
Not whether it is fine.

15
00:00:47,131 --> 00:00:48,241
Whether it is yours.

16
00:00:48,241 --> 00:00:52,847
The system message gets you most of the way there, and the trial's read-every-draft

17
00:00:52,847 --> 00:00:55,122
fortnight is where you sand off the rest.

18
00:00:55,672 --> 00:01:00,548
Read it as a client would, not as a builder: nothing in it is clever, and everything

19
00:01:00,548 --> 00:01:04,205
in it is a boundary you decided before the first draft existed.

20
00:01:04,205 --> 00:01:06,701
The next slide takes it apart line by line.

21
00:01:07,251 --> 00:01:11,289
Three sentences in that prompt are doing three different kinds of work.

22
00:01:11,289 --> 00:01:13,962
One controls content: name the client and date.

23
00:01:13,962 --> 00:01:18,569
One controls tone: no threats, no premium figure sitting in a client's inbox as a

24
00:01:18,569 --> 00:01:20,161
number they did not ask for.

25
00:01:20,161 --> 00:01:24,938
One controls the agent's own self-understanding: it is drafting, not sending, and it

26
00:01:24,938 --> 00:01:27,440
should never write as though it already has.

27
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:31,478
That last sentence sounds philosophical and is actually very practical.

28
00:01:31,478 --> 00:01:36,255
A drafting model that believes it is mid-correspondence invents history: "further to

29
00:01:36,255 --> 00:01:40,862
my last email", when there was no last email, or "as discussed", when nothing was

30
00:01:40,862 --> 00:01:41,431
discussed.

31
00:01:41,431 --> 00:01:45,525
Every one of those phrases is a small lie your client can catch, and the

32
00:01:45,525 --> 00:01:48,255
never-write-as-sent line is what keeps them out.

33
00:01:48,255 --> 00:01:52,975
The missing information clause works the same corner: if a row has no contact name,

34
00:01:52,975 --> 00:01:57,753
the honest draft opens generically and Priya fixes it in four seconds, which beats a

35
00:01:57,753 --> 00:02:00,767
warm "Dear Sam" addressed to a Sam the model made up.

36
00:02:00,767 --> 00:02:05,373
Same escape hatch logic as Section 4, applied to correspondence: give the model a

37
00:02:05,373 --> 00:02:07,250
dignified way to not know things.

38
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:09,214
That is the whole prompt.

39
00:02:09,214 --> 00:02:13,797
It reads shorter than Section 5's, and it should: the hard part of this build was

40
00:02:13,797 --> 00:02:14,816
never the writing.

41
00:02:14,816 --> 00:02:19,455
It was deciding, in advance, exactly what a client should never see, and saying so

42
00:02:19,455 --> 00:02:24,095
in one sentence rather than trusting the model to guess your standards on its own.

43
00:02:24,095 --> 00:02:28,791
And notice the stronger ban hiding in plain sight: the User Message never hands the

44
00:02:28,791 --> 00:02:29,922
premium over at all.

45
00:02:29,922 --> 00:02:34,562
The filter passes client, contact, date and days remaining, and the Premium column

46
00:02:34,562 --> 00:02:36,033
stays behind in the sheet.

47
00:02:36,033 --> 00:02:39,993
The prompt's "do not mention" line is the second fence, not the first.

48
00:02:39,993 --> 00:02:44,520
That two-layer pattern, exclude the data, then also forbid the mention, is worth

49
00:02:44,520 --> 00:02:49,272
stealing for anything sensitive: a model cannot leak a number it never received, and

50
00:02:49,272 --> 00:02:53,799
the instruction only has to catch the case where the number sneaks in some other

51
00:02:53,799 --> 00:02:54,025
way.

52
00:02:54,025 --> 00:02:55,722
Wiring first, briefing second.

53
00:02:55,722 --> 00:02:57,985
By now that order should sound familiar.

54
00:02:58,535 --> 00:03:03,087
Test the tone the cheap way before the next lecture: pin your filtered rows, run the

55
00:03:03,087 --> 00:03:07,097
agent, and read its three drafts aloud, in your voice, as if signing them.

56
00:03:07,097 --> 00:03:11,161
Mark anything you would rephrase, tighten the briefing, run again for free.

57
00:03:11,161 --> 00:03:15,712
Tone converges fast under that loop, usually in two or three passes, because you are

58
00:03:15,712 --> 00:03:17,446
not teaching the model to write.

59
00:03:17,446 --> 00:03:20,155
You are teaching it to stop writing like software.

60
00:03:20,155 --> 00:03:22,160
The draft now reads the way you want.

61
00:03:22,160 --> 00:03:26,333
Next lecture is where you make sure it can never do anything except sit there

62
00:03:26,333 --> 00:03:27,308
waiting for Priya.
